    "text": "Every year people throw away millions of phones and laptops, and much of this waste ends up in developing countries. As a [[1]] at a recycling plant, Omar knows that the problem is [[2]]: it will not disappear until the whole industry changes the way it works. His plant uses [[3]] sorting machines that decide in seconds which parts can be reused. The records of where every device goes are checked by an independent [[4]], and every label must be [[5]] by the machines' cameras. Households can leave old equipment at a [[6]] point, which prevents [[7]] metals such as lead from reaching the soil. Burning cables in the open also releases dangerous carbon emissions. Omar's team also repairs some phones and sells them cheaply to local schools. \"Nothing is thrown away if it can be used again,\" he says with a smile. The metals that are recovered are [[8]], so recycling makes economic sense as well.",
