    "text": "Ten years ago, Cresby was the kind of town people drove through without slowing down. The factory that had employed half the population had closed, shop windows were covered with boards, and the young left as soon as they could. Then a retired teacher named Olga Brandt had an idea that many of her neighbours thought was ridiculous. [[1]] Her plan was to cover the grey walls of the old high street with paintings.\n\nThe first mural, a giant heron on the side of the bakery, appeared in a single weekend. Olga had begged the owner for permission, and a local art student had drawn the outline. [[2]] By Sunday evening even the sceptics had stopped to watch. The heron became a bone of contention for a while, with some people calling it beautiful and others calling it a waste of paint, but nobody could ignore it.\n\nWord spread quickly. A photograph of the heron appeared online, and within a month visitors were arriving by coach. [[3]] She therefore organised an advertising campaign, with posters in the nearby cities, and invited artists from abroad to add new work. Soon the town's cafés were so busy that two of them had to take on extra staff.\n\nNot everybody was delighted. A few shopkeepers complained that the paintings drew attention away from their windows, and one letter to the local newspaper called the whole project childish. Some residents had strong feelings about the changes, especially when the artists began painting scenes of local life that were not always flattering. [[4]] Another sent up the mayor himself, showing him as a puppet on strings held by the council's accountants. He fell out with Olga for almost a year, although the two are friends again now.\n\nYet the benefits were hard to deny. Shops that had stood empty for years found new owners, and house prices rose after decades of decline. [[5]] Olga often points to this when doubters ask whether paint can really change a place. \"The vast majority of people here now say it has,\" she says, \"and the figures agree with them. That is the important point.\"\n\nToday there are more than a hundred murals in Cresby, and new ones are added every summer. Visitors come from all over the country, and some stay for a week to follow the trail of paintings from one end of the town to the other. [[6]] \"I'll keep going until they have to carry me down from the ladder,\" she says. \"Walls don't paint themselves.\"",
