    "intro": "You are going to read about four teenagers who describe their first job. For questions 1-10, choose from the people (A-D). The people may be chosen more than once.",
    "stem": "Which teenager…",
    "sections": [
     {
      "l": "A",
      "h": "Lucia, 16 — café assistant",
      "t": "I work at weekends in a café by the beach. My main job is carrying trays, but I also have to tidy away the chairs and wipe the tables once the lunch rush is over. On my second day I put my foot in it: I asked a woman when her baby was due, and she wasn't pregnant. I wanted to disappear! My manager only laughed and told me that everybody does something like that once, which made me feel a lot better. What I enjoy most is that customers come from all over the world, so I've learnt a lot about cultural differences. Some tourists leave huge tips and others almost nothing, and it has little to do with whether they liked the coffee. The café is busy but friendly, and I've made two good friends among the other waiters. I'm saving for a laptop, although my mum thinks I should put the money aside for university."
     },
     {
      "l": "B",
      "h": "Tomás, 15 — dog walker",
      "t": "I started walking dogs for our neighbours last summer, mainly because I wanted a better bike than my friends had, and I'll admit I was trying to keep up with the Joneses. That changed quickly. The dogs need so much exercise that I burn off more energy in an hour with them than in a whole football practice. The hardest part was talking to the owners about money. I used to beat around the bush instead of simply saying what the price was, until my sister told me I sounded as if I was apologising. Now I say it straight. I fell out with one customer who wanted me to take four dogs out together for the price of one, and I refused, but the vast majority of the owners are really kind and often give me a biscuit too."
     },
     {
      "l": "C",
      "h": "Hana, 17 — hotel helper",
      "t": "I work at my aunt's small hotel in the old town. When guests check out, I clear out the rooms, strip the beds and put everything back in order. I thought it would be boring, but I've found it needs practical skills that nobody ever taught me at school: folding sheets so they look perfect, mending a dripping tap, even calming a guest who has lost her passport. I'm not someone who plans everything, so I go with the flow when a coach party arrives without warning. The only part I really dislike is the 6 a.m. start; I'd happily work until midnight instead. At the end of the day my aunt gives me a free dinner in the hotel restaurant, which is a good reward."
     },
     {
      "l": "D",
      "h": "Ravi, 14 — market stall",
      "t": "I help my father on his fruit and vegetable stall at the Saturday market, and it's my first proper job. At the beginning I felt silly shouting out prices to get people to stop, and my voice went all squeaky. Now I can do it without thinking. I've also become much quicker at adding up bills in my head, because my father won't let me use the calculator. The market is noisy and crowded, and I love the smell of the oranges. Customers are friendly, particularly the regulars, who often ask what's best that week. One day I want to run a business of my own, perhaps a shop selling fresh juice, and the stall is teaching me how it works. My father gives me a small share of what we earn, and I give part of it to my mum."
     }
    ],
